### Where Matchers Are Built

The `RegExpRouter` builds its matchers primarily within the `buildAllMatchers` method. This process involves several key functions and classes:

1.  **`RegExpRouter.buildAllMatchers()`**: This is the main entry point for building matchers. It iterates over all HTTP methods for which routes have been added and calls `#buildMatcher` for each one. After building, it clears the internal route and middleware caches.
    `src/router/reg-exp-router/router.ts:208-222`
    ```typescript
    protected buildAllMatchers(): MatcherMap<T> {
      const matchers: MatcherMap<T> = Object.create(null)

      Object.keys(this.#routes!)
        .concat(Object.keys(this.#middleware!))
        .forEach((method) => {
          matchers[method] ||= this.#buildMatcher(method)
        })

      // Release cache
      this.#middleware = this.#routes = undefined
      clearWildcardRegExpCache()

      return matchers
    }
    ```

2.  **`RegExpRouter.#buildMatcher()`**: This private method gathers all routes and middleware for a specific HTTP method. It then passes this collection of routes to `buildMatcherFromPreprocessedRoutes`.
    `src/router/reg-exp-router/router.ts:224-251`

3.  **`buildMatcherFromPreprocessedRoutes()`**: This function takes the preprocessed routes, sorts them, and uses a `Trie` to construct a single, large regular expression that can match all dynamic routes. Static routes are stored separately in a `staticMap` for faster lookups.
    `src/router/reg-exp-router/router.ts:34-103`
    ```typescript
    function buildMatcherFromPreprocessedRoutes<T>(
      routes: [string, HandlerWithMetadata<T>[]][]
    ): Matcher<T> {
      const trie = new Trie()
      // ...
      try {
        paramAssoc = trie.insert(path, j, pathErrorCheckOnly)
      } catch (e) {
        throw e === PATH_ERROR ? new UnsupportedPathError(path) : e
      }
      // ...
      const [regexp, indexReplacementMap, paramReplacementMap] = trie.buildRegExp()
      // ...
      return [regexp, handlerMap, staticMap] as Matcher<T>
    }
    ```

4.  **`Trie.buildRegExp()`**: This method traverses the trie structure and generates the final regular expression string, which is then compiled into a `RegExp` object.
    `src/router/reg-exp-router/trie.ts:49-73`

### When Matchers Are Built

The matcher building process is performed **lazily**, occurring only on the **first call** to the `router.match(method, path)` method.

1.  The `RegExpRouter` class is assigned a `match` method from `src/router/reg-exp-router/matcher.ts`.
    `src/router/reg-exp-router/router.ts:206`
    ```typescript
    match: typeof match<Router<T>, T> = match
    ```

2.  On its first invocation, this `match` function calls `(this as any).buildAllMatchers()` to generate and cache the matchers.
    `src/router/reg-exp-router/matcher.ts:10-12`
    ```typescript
    export function match<R extends Router<T>, T>(this: R, method: string, path: string): Result<T> {
      // eslint-disable-next-line @typescript-eslint/no-explicit-any
      const matchers: MatcherMap<T> = (this as any).buildAllMatchers()
    ```

3.  After building the matchers, the function reassigns `this.match` to an inner function that uses the now-cached matchers for all subsequent calls. This ensures the expensive build process only runs once.
    `src/router/reg-exp-router/matcher.ts:31-32`
    ```typescript
      this.match = match
